  note         = "Accessed: 2025-11-2",
  language     = "en",
  annote       = "``Alex'' is the pseudonym used for an anonymous employee of
                  the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons
                  (OPCW) who became involved in a series of information leaks
                  concerning the OPCW's investigation into the chemical attack
                  on Douma, Syria in April 2018. While the true identity of
                  ``Alex'' has not been made public, several people claim to
                  have met and spoken with this individual, making it unlikely
                  that ``Alex'' is a fictional character. Alex's involvement
                  centers on their criticism and complaints regarding the OPCW's
                  draft and interim reports about the Douma attack. His
                  concerns, primarily about the technical and chemical findings
                  and the way information was handled, were highlighted in
                  documents leaked to WikiLeaks. However, analyses--like the one
                  from Bellingcat--suggest that many of Alex's claims are
                  misleading, taken out of context, or contradicted by
                  authoritative OPCW final reports. The situation is further
                  complicated by broader geopolitical pressures and selective
                  document releases by WikiLeaks. In summary, ``Alex''
                  represents a whistleblower figure within the OPCW whose
                  allegations and leaks sparked significant controversy, debate,
                  and scrutiny of the OPCW's processes and findings about the
                  Douma incident."
